--- Mrs. Ada Turner's diary, Friday, the 2nd of January, 1931 (3/4 key facts kept) ---
I pawned my late husband's silver watch for ten shillings. Bert gave me half his week's earnings, and I live in hopes. I read that more than two and a half million men are out of work, and the coal and cotton trades are in dispute. I also read that a general strike has thrown 150,000 miners out of work in South Wales, and optimists hope that a temporary arrangement may be made to let them go back to the pits. Finally, I paid Mr. Gedge seven shillings for rent.

--- Mr. Arthur Hale's diary, Friday, the 2nd of January, 1931 (1/2 key facts kept) ---
Friday, January 2nd, 1931. To-day I entered a wrong figure in the ledger, and spoke to Mr. Walter Thomas about telling him of it, and letting myself be fined a shilling.

--- Mr. Walter Thomas's diary, Friday, the 2nd of January, 1931 (0/2 key facts kept) ---
I read in The Times that the New Year opens gloomily, with more than two and a half million persons out of work, and the coal and cotton trades in dispute; I read also that a general strike in the South Wales coal-mining area has thrown 150,000 miners out of work, and that hopeful expectations are cherished in London of an arrangement which will permit these men to return to their occupations; I spoke with Mr. Arthur Hale about an error of his, whereby he let himself be fined a shilling.

