--- Mrs. Ada Turner's diary, Sunday, the 4th of January, 1931 (0/1 key facts kept) ---
To-day I went down to the river-stairs in despair, and contemplated suicide; but a kindly police-constable found me, and walked me home, and Mrs. Nolan came in and sat with me a long while.

--- Mr. Arthur Hale's diary, Sunday, the 4th of January, 1931 (0/2 key facts kept) ---
Sunday, January 4th. To-day I counted up what I had in hand, and found £3 14s. 3d.

--- Mr. Walter Thomas's diary, Sunday, the 4th of January, 1931 (3/3 key facts kept) ---
To-day I set Mr. Hale to balance the quarterly ledger, and spoke to him about telling me of his error, that I might fine him a shilling. He was sorry, and asked to be fined. I examined the quarterly ledger, kept by him, and found it not finished; so I resolved that in future they shall be finished by Tuesday evening.
   ☾ Ada: She dreamt that she was standing on London Bridge, and saw the great city all alight from end to end; and in the midst of the shining she beheld a splendid palace, and heard a voice saying, "This is for thee."

